			<div class="content" id="post-content-3589322">
									

<blockquote>
<p>a group composed of intelligent dolphin</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Intelligent dolphins?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3477-19 is noncorporeal humanoid entity</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A noncorporeal entity?</p>
<p>I have issues with the conceit that each would react in precisely the same way to stimulus excepting those that involve their immortality of choice. Each would've undergone mental and physical changes as a result of these alterations, and surely as they continued developing in different directions they imbibed different ideologies, personalities and beliefs. They're different buildings working off of the same type of foundation - unless I'm missing something intrinsically essential to the nature of the fact that there is more than one of them.</p>
<p>Other than that is fucking weird in a way I really like. It's a bona fide anomaly, in that the starting conditions make little sense but you proceed from there logically and the story ends up being this epic story about mortality and human fragility. I think I love this? The individual stories are just so out-there and entertaining. One Sarkic Harold, one Churchie, fighting each other in Australia. I can think of nothing else out there quite like this. It makes me think of Gazers, only because that one was also so bizarre and barely coherent it ended up being glorious.</p>
<p>All the +1's, this is excellent.</p>

