			<div class="content" id="post-content-3590485">
									

<p>The more I thought about that part of the SCP, the more I liked it in particular.</p>
<p>My headcanon for this story solves Kakroom's concern (at least in my opinion - they may disagree) by saying that all instances of 3477 were anomalous from the start. In particular, I'm thinking they're not exact copies, but are instead imperfect and incomplete. They all independently try to mimic what the real Harold would have done. In a way, maybe they're both not very good at imitating the real Harold, and a little <em>too</em> good at it. They talk in unison because it's a perfect recitation of things Harold might say, even though they're behaving in a way that's completely unnatural since the real Harold would never have been confronted with dozens of duplicates of himself.</p>
<p>This is motivated by additional headcanon that goes along with some other stories around here that say that immortality isn't actually possible, and that every attempt to become immortal always has some undesired outcome, despite never actually granting immortality. Maybe the 3477s can only gain &quot;immortality&quot; because they're artificial in the first place. Perhaps the duplicates were created and directed to undertake a variety of techniques for immortality as part of some experiment directed by someone else (maybe even the original Harold, on a quest to gain immortality for himself in a way that actually works without being completely awful to endure)?</p>

